 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Inputs:** A set of multi-view images (e.g., LLFF dataset) with known camera poses; a pre-computed 3D feature volume $V_s$ [S1]; ray bundles constructed from image pixels and camera poses [S2].
-*   **Outputs:** Optimized network parameters for the coarse network $\hat{C}_c$ and fine network $\hat{C}_f$; rendered images with photometric loss minimized [S1, S2].
+*   **Inputs:** Multi-view images (e.g., LLFF datasets or laparoscopic video frames), camera poses, and a pre-computed volume $V_s$ containing sampled features.
+*   **Outputs:** Optimized network parameters for coarse ($\hat{C}_c$) and fine ($\hat{C}_f$) networks, producing rendered images with color $\mathbf{c}$ and opacity $\sigma$.
 
 **Data Structures:**
-*   **Feature Volume ($V_s$):** A 3D grid storing 64-dimensional feature vectors. Each vector $\mathbf{v}_s$ is obtained via trilinear sampling $S(V_s, \mathbf{p})$ at point $\mathbf{p} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ [S1].
-*   **Ray Bundle:** A tensor of shape $(B, 3)$ representing ray origins and directions, where $B$ is the batch size [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Sample Points:** A tensor of shape $(B, N, 3)$ representing 3D coordinates along rays, where $N$ is the number of samples per ray [S1].
+*   **Volume $V_s$:** A 3D volume storing 64-dimensional feature vectors.
+*   **Feature Vector $\mathbf{v}_s$:** A 64-dimensional vector obtained via trilinear sampling $S(V_s, \mathbf{p})$ at point $\mathbf{p} \in \mathbb{R}^3$. No positional encoding is applied to $\mathbf{v}_s$ [S1].
+*   **Direction Vector $\mathbf{d}$:** A 3D vector representing ray direction.
+*   **Encoded Direction $\gamma(\mathbf{d})$:** A higher-dimensional vector derived from $\mathbf{d}$ using positional encoding [S1].
 
